1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,214
Two minutes.

2
00:00:04,334 --> 00:00:09,694
That is roughly how long Abraham
Lincoln spoke at Gettysburg in 1863,

3
00:00:09,734 --> 00:00:13,231
after the main speaker had
talked for about two hours.

4
00:00:13,409 --> 00:00:16,150
Five copies survive in his own hand.

5
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:20,189
Only one, known as the Bliss
copy, is signed and dated.

6
00:00:20,229 --> 00:00:22,162
It is kept in the White House.

7
00:00:22,281 --> 00:00:24,932
The man who wrote it
had, by his own count,

8
00:00:24,972 --> 00:00:27,041
less than a year of schooling.

9
00:00:37,355 --> 00:00:41,160
Abraham Lincoln was born on
the twelfth of February, 1809,

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,374
in a one-room log cabin on
his father's farm in Kentucky.

11
00:00:45,616 --> 00:00:49,096
His parents were Thomas
Lincoln, a carpenter and farmer,

12
00:00:49,136 --> 00:00:50,769
and Nancy Hanks.

13
00:00:50,959 --> 00:00:55,669
When he was seven, the family moved
to Indiana, partly, he later said,

14
00:00:55,709 --> 00:00:59,597
on account of slavery, but
chiefly over land titles.

15
00:00:59,669 --> 00:01:02,778
An axe was put into his hands at once.

16
00:01:07,318 --> 00:01:11,945
When he was nine, his mother died
of what people called milk sickness.

17
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:16,125
His stepmother, Sarah Bush
Johnston, proved, in his words,

18
00:01:16,165 --> 00:01:18,088
"a good and kind mother".

19
00:01:18,326 --> 00:01:21,904
He wrote of himself, "What he
has in the way of education,

20
00:01:21,944 --> 00:01:31,298
he has picked up." At nineteen,

21
00:01:31,338 --> 00:01:36,321
Lincoln helped take a flatboat down the
Ohio and Mississippi to New Orleans,

22
00:01:36,361 --> 00:01:38,458
over twelve hundred miles.

23
00:01:38,547 --> 00:01:41,653
In 1830 the family moved on to Illinois,

24
00:01:41,693 --> 00:01:44,509
where he split rails to fence ten acres.

25
00:01:44,593 --> 00:01:48,302
Rails like these later became
part of his campaign image.

26
00:01:48,543 --> 00:01:50,383
On a second trip to New Orleans,

27
00:01:50,423 --> 00:01:54,232
he would have seen slavery on
a scale unknown in Illinois.

28
00:01:54,272 --> 00:01:58,041
Stories of its effect on him
came only after his death;

29
00:01:58,142 --> 00:02:00,311
historians treat them with caution.

30
00:02:00,699 --> 00:02:04,962
At New Salem he was a store
clerk, postmaster and surveyor.

31
00:02:05,092 --> 00:02:09,200
In the Black Hawk War, his fellow
volunteers elected him captain.

32
00:02:09,282 --> 00:02:13,435
He saw no battle, but said
nothing later satisfied him more.

33
00:02:18,042 --> 00:02:23,261
In 1834, Lincoln was elected to
the Illinois legislature as a Whig.

34
00:02:23,529 --> 00:02:26,279
He borrowed law books from John T.

35
00:02:26,319 --> 00:02:29,234
Stuart, later his law
partner, and taught himself.

36
00:02:29,432 --> 00:02:31,930
"He studied with nobody," he wrote.

37
00:02:32,234 --> 00:02:38,011
In 1837 he moved to Springfield with
all his belongings in two saddlebags.

38
00:02:38,147 --> 00:02:42,141
That year he joined a protest
against slavery in the legislature.

39
00:02:42,325 --> 00:02:44,622
He met Mary Todd at a ball.

40
00:02:46,262 --> 00:02:51,264
Their engagement broke off, and
in January 1841 Lincoln wrote,

41
00:02:51,352 --> 00:02:54,216
"I am now the most miserable man living."

42
00:02:54,256 --> 00:02:57,540
Friends spoke of his
bouts of deep melancholy.

43
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:02,748
But in November 1842 they married,
and they would have four sons.

44
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:05,634
He rode the legal circuit for years.

45
00:03:07,274 --> 00:03:11,642
In 1846 he was elected to
Congress for a single term,

46
00:03:11,682 --> 00:03:14,942
where he opposed the war
with Mexico and proposed a

47
00:03:14,982 --> 00:03:18,628
bill to begin ending slavery
in the District of Columbia.

48
00:03:23,706 --> 00:03:25,358
In 1854,

49
00:03:25,398 --> 00:03:27,925
the Kansas–Nebraska Act
reopened the question

50
00:03:27,965 --> 00:03:30,508
of slavery in the western territories,

51
00:03:30,548 --> 00:03:34,927
and drew Lincoln back into politics,
in the new Republican Party.

52
00:03:35,166 --> 00:03:40,655
In June 1858, running for the Senate,
he quoted the Bible in a speech.

53
00:03:40,695 --> 00:03:43,435
"A house divided against itself cannot

54
00:03:43,475 --> 00:03:46,973
stand." "I believe this
government cannot endure,

55
00:03:47,013 --> 00:03:49,905
permanently half slave
and half free." He met

56
00:03:49,945 --> 00:03:54,738
the Democrat Stephen Douglas in
seven debates across Illinois.

57
00:03:54,778 --> 00:03:58,970
At Charleston he said something
hard to hear today: "I am not,

58
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:00,306
nor ever have been,

59
00:04:00,346 --> 00:04:05,785
in favor of bringing about in any way
the social and political equality of

60
00:04:05,825 --> 00:04:11,020
the white and black races…" He also
opposed Black voting and jury service.

61
00:04:11,211 --> 00:04:15,359
The legislature, which chose
senators then, returned Douglas.

62
00:04:15,453 --> 00:04:16,839
Lincoln lost.

63
00:04:16,879 --> 00:04:18,978
But the debates made his name,

64
00:04:19,018 --> 00:04:23,691
and in February 1860 he spoke
at Cooper Union in New York:

65
00:04:23,813 --> 00:04:27,021
"let us have faith that
right makes might".

66
00:04:31,561 --> 00:04:36,282
In May 1860, the Republicans
chose him to run for President.

67
00:04:36,448 --> 00:04:40,107
That October, an eleven-year-old
girl, Grace Bedell,

68
00:04:40,147 --> 00:04:42,015
wrote to him about a beard.

69
00:04:42,147 --> 00:04:43,908
Apparently taking her advice,

70
00:04:43,948 --> 00:04:46,614
he became the first President to wear one.

71
00:04:46,826 --> 00:04:49,729
In November he won a four-way race.

72
00:04:49,782 --> 00:04:53,603
Many white Southerners feared
he would move against slavery.

73
00:04:53,643 --> 00:04:57,404
Before he took office, seven
states had left the Union,

74
00:04:57,444 --> 00:04:59,090
South Carolina first,

75
00:04:59,130 --> 00:05:02,458
and formed the Confederacy
under Jefferson Davis.

76
00:05:02,656 --> 00:05:07,412
Slavery, and the fight over its
spread, lay at the root of the crisis.

77
00:05:07,463 --> 00:05:09,436
His election was the spark.

78
00:05:09,819 --> 00:05:12,286
On the twelfth of April, 1861,

79
00:05:12,326 --> 00:05:16,655
Confederate forces attacked Fort
Sumter, in Charleston harbour.

80
00:05:16,695 --> 00:05:21,638
It surrendered, and Lincoln called
for seventy-five thousand volunteers.

81
00:05:21,747 --> 00:05:23,516
The war had begun.

82
00:05:28,057 --> 00:05:30,773
Early on came defeat at Bull Run.

83
00:05:30,956 --> 00:05:34,331
In February 1862, his son
Willie died, aged eleven.

84
00:05:34,371 --> 00:05:36,760
In August he wrote publicly,

85
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:41,642
"My paramount object in this
struggle is to save the Union,

86
00:05:41,682 --> 00:05:45,139
and is not either to save
or to destroy slavery."

87
00:05:45,179 --> 00:05:49,458
Yet he had already shown his
Cabinet a draft proclamation.

88
00:05:49,498 --> 00:05:53,542
On the first of January
1863, as a war measure,

89
00:05:53,582 --> 00:05:57,524
the Emancipation Proclamation
declared that all persons held

90
00:05:57,564 --> 00:05:59,957
as slaves in the rebel states "are,

91
00:05:59,997 --> 00:06:02,131
and henceforward shall be free".

92
00:06:02,415 --> 00:06:06,064
It could not free anyone
beyond the Union army's reach,

93
00:06:06,104 --> 00:06:09,725
and left slavery untouched
in the loyal border states.

94
00:06:09,814 --> 00:06:13,280
But the war was now a war against slavery.

95
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:16,747
It also opened the army
and navy to Black men;

96
00:06:16,899 --> 00:06:19,554
nearly two hundred thousand served.

97
00:06:19,688 --> 00:06:24,041
Frederick Douglass recruited soldiers,
and two of his sons enlisted.

98
00:06:24,235 --> 00:06:27,416
In July the Union won at
Gettysburg, at terrible cost.

99
00:06:27,456 --> 00:06:28,768
That August,

100
00:06:28,861 --> 00:06:33,060
Frederick Douglass protested to Lincoln
that Black soldiers were paid less.

101
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:37,887
In November, Lincoln helped
dedicate a cemetery there.

102
00:06:41,427 --> 00:06:43,298
The signed copy begins:

103
00:06:43,424 --> 00:06:48,351
"Four score and seven years ago our
fathers brought forth on this continent,

104
00:06:48,391 --> 00:06:51,260
a new nation, conceived in Liberty,

105
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:54,034
and dedicated to the proposition that all

106
00:06:54,074 --> 00:07:01,933
men are created equal." In April 1864,

107
00:07:01,973 --> 00:07:05,955
Lincoln wrote, "I am
naturally anti-slavery.

108
00:07:06,038 --> 00:07:07,674
If slavery is not wrong,

109
00:07:07,714 --> 00:07:11,841
nothing is wrong." That
summer he expected to lose.

110
00:07:11,921 --> 00:07:16,952
Then Union forces took Atlanta,
and in November he was re-elected.

111
00:07:17,236 --> 00:07:21,798
In January 1865, the House
passed the Thirteenth Amendment,

112
00:07:21,838 --> 00:07:23,503
which he had pressed for.

113
00:07:25,543 --> 00:07:28,703
That March, in his Second
Inaugural, he said,

114
00:07:28,766 --> 00:07:32,387
"With malice toward none;
with charity for all;

115
00:07:32,497 --> 00:07:36,431
with firmness in the right, as
God gives us to see the right,

116
00:07:36,471 --> 00:07:39,651
let us strive on to
finish the work we are in;

117
00:07:39,707 --> 00:07:42,400
to bind up the nation's wounds…" Frederick

118
00:07:42,440 --> 00:07:46,167
Douglass recalled calling
it "a sacred effort".

119
00:07:46,207 --> 00:07:49,764
On the ninth of April, at
Appomattox Court House, Robert E.

120
00:07:49,804 --> 00:07:53,561
Lee surrendered his Confederate
army to the Union commander,

121
00:07:53,601 --> 00:07:54,679
Ulysses S.

122
00:07:54,719 --> 00:07:58,456
Grant. An estimated six
hundred and twenty thousand to

123
00:07:58,496 --> 00:08:01,683
seven hundred and fifty
thousand died in the war.

124
00:08:06,419 --> 00:08:10,006
Five days later, on the evening
of the fourteenth of April,

125
00:08:10,046 --> 00:08:13,740
Lincoln was at Ford's Theatre
in Washington, watching a play,

126
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:15,311
Our American Cousin.

127
00:08:15,501 --> 00:08:18,724
At about a quarter past
ten, John Wilkes Booth,

128
00:08:18,764 --> 00:08:23,852
a Confederate sympathiser and racist
who despised his efforts to end slavery,

129
00:08:23,892 --> 00:08:25,302
shot the President.

130
00:08:25,445 --> 00:08:28,045
Lincoln never regained consciousness.

131
00:08:30,085 --> 00:08:33,638
He was carried across the street
to the Petersen boarding house,

132
00:08:33,678 --> 00:08:37,075
and laid diagonally across
a bed in a small back room,

133
00:08:37,115 --> 00:08:39,526
because he was too tall to lie straight.

134
00:08:39,761 --> 00:08:43,073
Mary waited in the front
parlour with their son Robert.

135
00:08:43,255 --> 00:08:46,150
At twenty-two minutes past
seven the next morning,

136
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:47,904
Abraham Lincoln died.

137
00:08:48,084 --> 00:08:49,593
He was fifty-six.

138
00:08:55,220 --> 00:09:00,097
His funeral train carried him one
thousand six hundred and fifty-four miles,

139
00:09:00,137 --> 00:09:03,156
through hundreds of towns,
home to Springfield.

140
00:09:03,322 --> 00:09:06,007
His son Willie's coffin
travelled with his.

141
00:09:06,169 --> 00:09:11,640
By December 1865, enough states had
ratified the Thirteenth Amendment.

142
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:14,871
Slavery was abolished
in the United States.

143
00:09:17,911 --> 00:09:21,700
In 1922 the Lincoln
Memorial was dedicated,

144
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:26,209
with the Gettysburg Address and the
Second Inaugural carved on its walls.

145
00:09:26,348 --> 00:09:27,566
At the dedication,

146
00:09:27,606 --> 00:09:31,780
Black guests were reportedly
seated in a separate section.

147
00:09:31,820 --> 00:09:34,280
Historians still argue about him.

148
00:09:34,361 --> 00:09:38,590
Some see a man who grew;
others, a man who was pushed.

149
00:09:38,726 --> 00:09:41,735
Frederick Douglass,
who knew him, saw both.

150
00:09:41,775 --> 00:09:44,490
The page he signed ends with a resolve:

151
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:48,725
"that government of the people,
by the people, for the people,

152
00:09:48,765 --> 00:09:53,857
shall not perish from the earth."
Two minutes, a few sheets of paper,

153
00:09:53,897 --> 00:09:56,461
and an argument that has never ended.

